#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171743 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16253771-4d04-5f82-97ab-830e05f9e15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality With One Linear Factor in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "6b20c76f-36a5-5cd2-bf15-1ce983d1d97c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5859" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{1-x}{x+2} < 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1-x}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\lt 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x) = 0$ is $x = 1.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x = -2.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad (-2,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-2\\right)$ | $\\left(-2,1\\right)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1-x$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) < 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty,-2)$ and $(1,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (1,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171744 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "f18c56c1-f989-582a-bbce-ce9566d4e72d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5861" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x-3} \\leq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-1}{x-3} .\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) =\t\\dfrac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x = \\pm 1.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-1) \\quad(-1,1) \\quad (1,3) \\quad (3,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-1\\right)$ | $\\left(-1,1\\right)$ | $\\left(1,3\\right)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty,-1)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x) \\leq 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty,-1]\\cup [1,3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171758 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "51b867ab-e66b-5c82-b729-2e600df813b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7749" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)} \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\geq 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x) = 0$ is $x=\\dfrac 12.$ Also, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x = -2$ and $x=1.$ The root and asymptotes divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\infty,-2\\right), \\quad \\left(-2,\\dfrac 12\\right), \\quad \\left(\\dfrac 12,1\\right), \\quad \\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-2\\right)$ | $\\left(-2,\\dfrac12\\right)$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12,1\\right)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) \\geq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $\\left(-2,\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $(1,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) \\geq 0$ is \n\n$$\nx\\in \\left(-2,\\dfrac12\\right]\\cup (1,\\infty).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16253771-4d04-5f82-97ab-830e05f9e15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality With One Linear Factor in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f25ce39-84e1-50dd-b888-8e36ef9e1f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291965} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2d026a-614a-54ea-a862-806b4b76d99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291966} {:answer/id #uuid "028d5493-4ac9-5b1f-a730-219b156efdea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{4})\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291967} {:answer/id #uuid "b94d748f-4873-5766-b113-6da9a45bee8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{5}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291968} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d76c42-b9de-52b9-96b3-4f4ebd131727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9d76c42-b9de-52b9-96b3-4f4ebd131727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291969} :answer-field/id #uuid "f02ca864-5ae2-52d7-bad1-1afeed27ae7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92a50d04-8f01-5c43-b9b8-1ea4815acd9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115810" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3-x}{4x-5}<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3-x}{4x-5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=3.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}),\\,(\\frac{5}{4},3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})$ | $(\\frac{5}{4},3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $4x-5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})\\cup (3,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f139b0e3-6c87-500b-a855-ed54af59c65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291972} {:answer/id #uuid "591c34cf-802e-5ad5-b249-93ac58b23f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291973} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb9d2f5-e58a-593c-9872-ead2c93ba613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291974} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac52963-46f3-5cc8-9977-0f8edbdae8fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291975} {:answer/id #uuid "a178568b-abd5-5e5f-9d8a-8cb8619ea5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f139b0e3-6c87-500b-a855-ed54af59c65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291972} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e399fe-c904-5769-9c9b-6452ee920671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "269a576b-c401-5abc-a7cf-817b5e5f8580" :question/math-academy-id "q-115832" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}+5}{(x+4)(3-x)}>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+5}{(x+4)(3-x)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe equation $f(x)=0$ has no solution. On the other hand, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptotes at $x=-4$ and $x=3.$ These asymptotes divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+5$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-4,3).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-4,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d6663b-2222-5240-b542-6ccb82fd7498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{7}{2},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291979} {:answer/id #uuid "0209ef1e-5f36-50ea-a891-1744993183bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [\\frac{7}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291980} {:answer/id #uuid "0d111e38-c083-5ecb-9e8f-5be0b08affb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2}]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291981} {:answer/id #uuid "3612cc5c-b462-592e-aa6a-1253f21602ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291982} {:answer/id #uuid "42639860-e085-5aca-9bc1-21ccfbaa83cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d111e38-c083-5ecb-9e8f-5be0b08affb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2}]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291981} :answer-field/id #uuid "9218bebb-8f1d-5bac-8c97-ccbf3e1d4463" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24fcd16f-61ca-5423-b124-6af6af808cac" :question/math-academy-id "q-115858" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{(2x+7)(3-x)}{{x}^{2}}\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(2x+7)(3-x)}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $x=3.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{7}{2},0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{7}{2},0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2})$ and $(3,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2}]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc72be4-3466-5430-9ce8-4474894c9ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-5,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291986} {:answer/id #uuid "3b86bdb5-bbce-54d6-9221-ece08dc61e4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291987} {:answer/id #uuid "0e39d66f-784b-553d-aa86-332fd98fc1ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291988} {:answer/id #uuid "fba5dd67-f5c0-5fd4-b041-70b89dd3594b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291989} {:answer/id #uuid "569f48cb-9521-5e81-be9c-fe6854117d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-5,-2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc72be4-3466-5430-9ce8-4474894c9ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-5,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291986} :answer-field/id #uuid "9598fdc9-a858-5d4b-a03d-901f79c31082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36ab03d9-4172-552d-aa1a-9362a50cffe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115985" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x+5}\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-2),\\,(-2,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-5,-2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-5,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16253771-4d04-5f82-97ab-830e05f9e15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality With One Linear Factor in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58d24def-f54c-5763-b95b-fa8e354ab970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58d24def-f54c-5763-b95b-fa8e354ab970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291993} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3031dae-4c9a-515d-82f4-9ce530867cb4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1783fe90-335f-5a13-9cf3-efa4b97766c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1783fe90-335f-5a13-9cf3-efa4b97766c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291995} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecd5668b-1d68-5c41-abfb-08cfddd5e3c2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51aa0942-474e-54ed-b9d0-5e0ebb621e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51aa0942-474e-54ed-b9d0-5e0ebb621e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291997} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6324a24-c52f-5a8d-9072-cbd492962d4a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2096393e-b75e-5b5f-b5b8-180daf222b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-274415" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{2x-3}{3x-2}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}\n$)⋃[$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{3x-2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into three subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3}),\\,(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3})$ | $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $3x-2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is nonnegative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$ Since the function equals zero at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we include this endpoint in the solution.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,)⋃[\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "693e7893-3964-53e8-9688-e95c1294ec9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292000} {:answer/id #uuid "856dee23-cfa4-50b1-91a1-abecc6369359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "856dee23-cfa4-50b1-91a1-abecc6369359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292001} :answer-field/id #uuid "e63c4d2b-8bdb-5929-92fa-32139690aea9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86b97f72-a094-57f6-ba1e-bd4ece62dcdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86b97f72-a094-57f6-ba1e-bd4ece62dcdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292003} :answer-field/id #uuid "10848d24-2032-594a-9280-2b22ed45f596" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8dae39b-5dc3-5782-b404-cf81501c2a12" :question/math-academy-id "q-274626" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}}{3-2x}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}}{3-2x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+1)}{3-2x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=0.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2{x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}\\,).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16253771-4d04-5f82-97ab-830e05f9e15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality With One Linear Factor in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf4d84af-0206-5b97-85de-5b66087d9512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf4d84af-0206-5b97-85de-5b66087d9512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292006} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c6db1ac-aa23-5653-bb27-23b164dee043" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292005} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1401baf8-7491-5741-a130-40d84c532447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1401baf8-7491-5741-a130-40d84c532447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292008} :answer-field/id #uuid "c68200df-2bfa-5ec3-98b0-a7dc12643e10" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a920c85e-6aa9-5e63-8475-d17a64777df2" :question/math-academy-id "q-324126" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{11-4x}{3-x}<0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{11-4x}{3-x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=\\frac{11}{4}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into three subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{11}{4}),\\,(\\frac{11}{4},3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{11}{4})$ | $(\\frac{11}{4},3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $11-4x$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(\\frac{11}{4},3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)<0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,\\frac{11}{4},\\,3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86fba9fe-b851-53ff-bba5-901e30b09e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86fba9fe-b851-53ff-bba5-901e30b09e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292011} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a2e99e-04d1-5af4-9ac9-11511cedbb54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78112087-3dee-5a7e-a2d2-329c9990c632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78112087-3dee-5a7e-a2d2-329c9990c632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292013} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d9ba9e3-863e-54c1-94f5-15d108007a0d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a638bce-dba7-54c4-9d86-d025e9cf926e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325754" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{-4}{{x}^{2}+7x}>0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{-4}{{x}^{2}+7x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{-4}{x(x+7)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)=0$ has no solution. On the other hand, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=-7$ and $x=0.$ These asymptotes divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-7),\\,(-7,0),\\,(0,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-7,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)>0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-7,0\\,).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56391d2-cd5b-53be-a63d-5cf30edcd35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292016} {:answer/id #uuid "be0f0714-41dc-5cb0-a351-3ae7ae1ef254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56391d2-cd5b-53be-a63d-5cf30edcd35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292016} :answer-field/id #uuid "009672d7-aabe-5f10-8b88-418ee309de54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6017ce45-4fee-51ce-8172-61d99294c54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6017ce45-4fee-51ce-8172-61d99294c54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292019} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca3211e6-3995-5e98-80ad-5d188a9540e2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4b10d23-31c8-5e8e-b6dd-43ac8cb510ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-326046" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{x-3}>0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $h(x)$ as\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $h(x)>0.$\n\nThe equation $h(x)=0$ has no solution because ${x}^{2}+1>0$ for all real $x.$ On the other hand, $h(x)$ has a single vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ This asymptote divides the number line into $2$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $h(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $h(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $h(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $h(x)$ is positive on the interval $(3,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nh(x)>0\\text{ for all }x\\in (\\,3,\\infty \\,).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4650e979-d2d5-58d6-bacb-e0b3de8edc76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{3},0)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292022} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0ea393-2389-5f01-afd2-2310c5a83a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292023} {:answer/id #uuid "012c8ee1-457b-5aca-85d9-2d0c1b4ecbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292024} {:answer/id #uuid "4f917a8b-27b9-5fec-8e97-ff3b79770e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292025} {:answer/id #uuid "597ecae0-59e3-5ee0-8fe7-9157957a394e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec0ea393-2389-5f01-afd2-2310c5a83a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292023} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb62559-8655-5a3c-aef7-c52a7b19663a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc751058-54b8-5d08-a921-6b30403f4883" :question/math-academy-id "q-38507" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3{x}^{2}+x}{x-2}<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}+x}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x(3x+1)}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=0.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{3},0),\\,(0,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{3},0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84f77394-4b07-5155-9d84-9871f0f1716b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-\\frac{3}{2})∪(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170870} {:answer/id #uuid "c62afe1e-4d81-523e-825e-06ffab083399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-2)∪(0, \\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170871} {:answer/id #uuid "146c3d11-1efe-5d5c-b7df-ab11660c8de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (\\frac{3}{2}, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170872} {:answer/id #uuid "2de572ae-6517-5bc8-aa60-fc1bd7f7f718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-2, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170873} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9d8d81-27b4-5c2b-87ea-2a0a73e0d320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-2, 0)∪(\\frac{3}{2}, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d9d8d81-27b4-5c2b-87ea-2a0a73e0d320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-2, 0)∪(\\frac{3}{2}, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170874} :answer-field/id #uuid "82926cb1-40a1-586c-bd66-b3a677e3f98e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59bb1ad4-d174-5fc9-b5c8-63da66cdfefc" :question/math-academy-id "q-38509" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-6}{x}>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-6}{x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x+2)(2x-3)}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-2,0)$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16253771-4d04-5f82-97ab-830e05f9e15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality With One Linear Factor in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6ecc5b6-942f-5ddd-8f29-6ed6fde45c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292029} {:answer/id #uuid "3e33e25f-8199-5fd5-acbb-8ac6d19a4007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292030} {:answer/id #uuid "fd27ff4e-a044-56de-a6da-b89a9a4fc545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292031} {:answer/id #uuid "77573b66-721d-513f-86a3-eca42f55b6d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292032} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc3aaee-25d5-5fce-b602-71389f5cf746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd27ff4e-a044-56de-a6da-b89a9a4fc545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292031} :answer-field/id #uuid "be23d0c1-a774-57d9-8525-1040c8034b4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0d0f9ac-c31b-5cb0-a5b9-34f876646895" :question/math-academy-id "q-88655" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{x-3}{x+1}<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-3}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=3.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1),\\,(-1,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(-1,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-1,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16253771-4d04-5f82-97ab-830e05f9e15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality With One Linear Factor in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ee87a2-9efe-5eef-8084-25bcfbf7731a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292036} {:answer/id #uuid "801f6367-49cd-513c-9bd3-5a46aeca454d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,-\\frac{1}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292037} {:answer/id #uuid "72f9c464-e920-5b73-bd8c-082ab168c4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292038} {:answer/id #uuid "46af4fb2-f2f6-539e-ad6f-251f02d33746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292039} {:answer/id #uuid "6574c863-9d14-5c58-8847-7be053c9955f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46af4fb2-f2f6-539e-ad6f-251f02d33746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292039} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b84b5f6-7f3f-584e-92c7-c52bd38020f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b338aae-74fc-5d1d-9908-3c10cfcf64bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-88656" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{x+2}{2x+1}\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=-2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$  The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a063a58c-7c61-576a-ae41-d75323e3e463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-1,-\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292043} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2fe62a-2878-5e49-9deb-93b4790f1e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,-\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292044} {:answer/id #uuid "791360d6-5492-5c4f-ad3e-609cccd67db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,-1)\\cup (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292045} {:answer/id #uuid "5a8e8732-4f50-5660-adef-5c312a12e40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{2}{3},1)\\cup (1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292046} {:answer/id #uuid "a03f4be0-3f5f-5083-b234-0d02f80015a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a063a58c-7c61-576a-ae41-d75323e3e463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-1,-\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292043} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d8c79bd-288b-5aa3-a0e6-67a31113f4eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72b7ff40-dcc6-57cf-bdfd-9c24ffb5cf65" :question/math-academy-id "q-88711" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{(x+1)(x+4)}{3x+2}<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x+1)(x+4)}{3x+2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,-1),\\,(-1,-\\frac{2}{3}),\\,(-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,-1)$ | $(-1,-\\frac{2}{3})$ | $(-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3x+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals  $(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(-1,-\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-1,-\\frac{2}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31c3301e-9546-5cf4-bf5e-5e37244bf670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292050} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3a891f-8e33-5154-b617-7a12edf4e9d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292051} {:answer/id #uuid "b45afb06-5628-5251-bf68-576c9be9b17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,\\frac{1}{2}]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292052} {:answer/id #uuid "dcea36f7-9a97-522e-a9d5-56871f651f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292053} {:answer/id #uuid "bce7993c-da0e-5e7e-b722-5103c1342d7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe3a891f-8e33-5154-b617-7a12edf4e9d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292051} :answer-field/id #uuid "801cf3ad-e340-5c9c-9c52-0ff0d72f297d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60ff8676-b37f-57cf-bd9e-eed47914a514" :question/math-academy-id "q-88712" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{(2x-1)(x-2)}{x+2}\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(2x-1)(x-2)}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},2).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},2].$"}]}
